[{"record_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003074","candidate_id":"full_2026-07-06__rec_003074","source_record_id":"rec_003074","source_corpus":"include","title":"Foundation model for single-cell omics","abstract":"Single-cell sequencing has enabled unprecedented characterization of distinct cell types and advanced our understanding of disease pathogenesis. The rapid expansion of single-cell data presents both tremendous opportunities and challenges. These rich datasets can reveal cellular heterogeneity, enable lineage tracking, and elucidate pathogenic mechanisms for developing personalized therapeutics. However, effectively analyzing the increasingly massive data also requires scalable computational approaches. As single-cell datasets grow exponentially, a critical need emerges to develop methodologies that can harness, enhance, and adapt to these burgeoning resources, and learning the representation for single-cell omics is one of the core tasks for understanding the exponentially growing sequencing data.\n\nThe thesis addresses this need by demonstrating a framework to extract meaningful patterns from the single-cell sequencing data. We develop versatile computational models capable of learning from large-scale data. The key innovation is a pretraining and transfer learning paradigm to construct foundation models tailored to single-cell analysis. We design self-supervised learning tasks to enable scalable training and pre-train the transformer foundation model, scGPT, on diverse unlabeled single-cell data to gain general capabilities. [truncated]","doi":"","year":"","venue":"","sources":[],"selected_full_text_sections":"[data_source: Continual pretraining and transfer learning for spatial-omics > 6.2 Continual pretraining of foundation model for spatial transcriptomics > 6.2.1 Data processing]\nWe employed a comprehensive approach to data acquisition and filtering, focusing on the integration of spatial transcriptomics data. This data was primarily sourced from the CELLxGENE Chanzuckerberg Initiative. [2022] platform and supplemented with datasets hosted on the Gene Expression Omnibus (GEO). Our selection criteria were specifically tailored to include only samples processed using Visium or Spatial Transcriptomics (ST) technologies and derived from human tissues. The resultant dataset of around 1 million Visium and ST sites from 278 individual studies, enriched with spatial context, offers a unique opportunity to delve into the complexities of cellular landscapes\n\nContrasting with the data collection strategy outlined in Section 3.2, which focused exclusively on normal (non-disease) conditions, this phase of data processing incorporated tissue samples from both normal and diseased states. The rationale behind this inclusive approach stems from the burgeoning potential and clinical relevance of spatial profiling technologies. These technologies have shown exceptional promise, particularly in oncological studies and other clinical research domains where the spatial context of gene expression provides critical insights into disease progression, tissue architecture, and cellular microenvironments.\n\nGiven the nascent yet rapidly evolving nature of spatial omics, including data from both healthy and diseased tissues enables us to capture a broader spectrum of cellular interactions and states. This dual-focus approach is especially pertinent in oncology, where understanding the spatial dynamics of tumor cells in relation to their microenvironment can yield groundbreaking insights into tumor biology, metastasis, and treatment responses. Furthermore, by encompassing a diverse array of conditions, we enhance the robustness and applicability of our models, ensuring they are well-equipped to handle a wide range of spatial transcriptomics data.\n\nIn processing this spatial omics data, we adopted quality control measures to ensure the integrity and reliability of the data. This involved filtering out samples with inadequate quality or insufficient metadata, thus maintaining a high standard of data quality.\n\n[input_representation: Continual pretraining and transfer learning for spatial-omics > 6.2 Continual pretraining of foundation model for spatial transcriptomics > 6.2.2 Continual pre-training methods > Adaptation of Input Embeddings for Spatial Omics]\nIn the process of continual pretraining for spatial omics, we have incorporated modifications to the input embedding strategy, as initially described in Section 3.3.1. This includes the integration of two new special tokens, which are specifically designed to address the observed high variability in sequencing depth and the number of genes across different sample slides and even within different regions of the same slide.\n\nIntegration of special Tokens To address the specificities of spatial omics data, we introduce two new special tokens: &lt; num of genes &gt; and &lt; total counts &gt; . These tokens are appended right after the &lt; cls &gt; token in the input sequence. The identifiers (ids) for these new tokens are added to the existing vocabulary, expanding the model's linguistic repertoire to better represent spatial transcriptomic data.\n\nThe &lt; num of genes &gt; token is designed to capture the count of sequenced genes with non-zero expressions in each individual spatial spot. It is encoded as an integer identifier, similar to the gene tokens. This token provides a quantitative measure of gene activity within a spatial sample, aiding the model in assessing the richness of the gene expression profile. Similarly, the &lt; total counts &gt; token encapsulates the total expression count of these genes before binning. This token offers a summary measure of overall gene expression intensity within a spot.\n\nRationale for New Tokens The rationale behind introducing these tokens is grounded in our observation of technical variations inherent in spatial omics data. Sequencing depth and gene count variability can significantly impact the model's ability to learn robust and accurate embeddings for spots and genes. By incorporating these new tokens, we provide the model with additional contextual information that aids in normalizing these technical variations, thereby enabling the generation of more consistent and reliable embeddings. The &lt; num of genes &gt; token helps the model account for the richness of the gene expression profile in a given spatial spot, while the &lt; total counts &gt; token provides a summary measure of overall gene expression intensity, crucial for understanding spatial patterns and distributions.\n\nUpdated Input Embedding Equation As per the original input embedding approach, each gene is assigned a unique identifier. For spatial transcriptomics, the gene tokens and their corresponding expression values are augmented with these new spatial-specific tokens, enriching the input data representation.\n\nThe final input embedding vector for each spatial spot is modified to include the embeddings for the new tokens, along with the existing gene tokens and condition tokens. The equation for the modified input vector is as follows:\n\n<!-- formula-not-decoded -->\n\nIn this updated equation, the identifiers for &lt; num of genes &gt; and &lt; total counts &gt; are appended right after the &lt; cls &gt; token, followed by the identifiers of the gene tokens. This structure ensures that the model receives the additional contextual information at the outset of processing each input spot.\n\nThe embedding layers are extended to process the new spatial-specific tokens alongside the traditional gene tokens and condition tokens. The final embedding for each spatial spot thus encompasses a comprehensive representation, integrating both the spatial context and the gene-level information."}]

